Z||000000||Hank is Here||10-17-2000||05:11 PM||||I know that this has probably been discussed before but is it possible and easy to convert a 88 2.5 DIS to a 2.5 distributor engine?  Are there differences in the head or block for the distrubutor?  Isn't there a problem with teh oil pan?   Thanks <BR>Mark Johnson <p>[This message has been edited by Hank is Here (edited 10-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||10-17-2000||10:51 PM||||Why in blazes would you want to. The DIS motor is far more stable and makes a little more power than the distributor motor.<P>You can't change one engine into the other. The head is the least of the problems.<P>you have to swap the whole engine and it's wiring and controls and the ECM. If you're going thru that much trouble it makes no sence to put in another 4, let alone a weaker one.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Hank is Here||10-17-2000||11:10 PM||||Okay, I have an 88 parts car and soon may be getting a 84 that needs an engine.  I was just wondering if I could use the 88 engine and convert it.  The 88 is way to far gone to ever be on the road.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-18-2000||08:01 PM||||It can be done. It's a hell of alot of work but it can be done. It'll be alot simpler if both cars have the same type tranny, IE stick or auto. Even then the 84 has so many wiring diferances it's not funny. Might be easier to transplant the 88's complete harness. (You'll want it's headlight system too.)<P>The issue with transmissions is 2 fold. The ECM has to have the right PROM and wiring for the tranny on hand. there's also a tranny mount issue.<P>If you take an automatic from an 88 and try to put it into an 84 that was stick you'd have to find diferant mounts. The 84 cradel won't take the single tranny mount setup used on 86.5+up automatics.||||reg||1
